.TH LEGBAR 1 "August 2026" "legbar 0.3.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
legbar \- one screen for the whole fleet: live agent sessions beside GitHub CI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B legbar
[\fB\-1\fR]
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-git\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-ci\fR]
[\fB\-i\fR \fISECS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B legbar
is a full-screen curses view that puts both fleet lanes on one canvas. The
SESSIONS pane lists every live agent on the machine; the CI / PRS pane lists
GitHub Actions runs and open pull requests across every clone, with failures
pinned so a red build cannot scroll away.
.PP
Both panes are drawn from a single discovery layer,
.BR henhouse ,
so the two views cannot disagree about what is running.
.BR henhouse (1)
is a working CLI in its own right.
.PP
Claude Code sessions are joined by pid against the live-session markers, with
model, context burn and status read from the session's own JSONL transcript.
Cursor agents appear too, marked by a
.BR cu-
prefix on the session name (Claude Code sessions take
.BR cc- ).
Their liveness is inferred rather than probed \- Cursor writes no pid to join
against, so a row means \fIthis transcript moved recently\fR, which is a weaker
signal and is labelled as one.
.PP
Read-only by construction: legbar reads transcripts and registries and runs
.BR gh
and read-only git plumbing. It never writes to a tree, a registry or a
session. Every data source is optional and degrades to a labelled gap rather
than an error \- an empty fleet renders an empty fleet, which is exactly the
case someone is looking at when they are debugging why nothing is running.
.PP
The output is ASCII only, on purpose: block-drawing characters mojibake in the
Windows console, so the context bars, pane rules and status glyphs are all
plain ASCII.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-once
Render one frame to stdout and exit. For pipes, CI logs and screenshots.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the joined state as JSON and exit, for piping somewhere else. The
object's first key is
.BR version ,
so a consumer can tell which schema it is reading without shelling out to
\fB\-\-version\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-git
Skip the per-tree git probes. Toggle at runtime with \fBg\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ci
Skip the gh sweep entirely. Useful offline, or when the API is slow.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colour in the full-screen view. \fBNO_COLOR\fR does the same.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval " " \fISECS\fR
Seconds between refreshes in the full-screen view (default 5).
.TP
.B \-\-waiting\-alert " " \fIMINUTES\fR
How long a conversation must sit unanswered before
.B NEEDS YOU
marks it with \fB!\fR and colours it (default 20). A younger wait is still
listed, just quiet \- a session waiting on you is the normal resting state of a
fleet, and flagging every one of them on sight teaches the eye to skip the
flag. \fB0\fR flags every wait. Contested trees are exempt and are loud from
the first frame.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B q / esc
quit
.TP
.B r
refresh everything now
.TP
.B g
toggle the per-tree git probes
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every override is \fBLEGBAR_*\fR. The older
.BR roost ,
.BR leghorn
and
.BR ccwork
variable names are still honoured so an existing install keeps whatever it had
configured; the new name wins when both are set.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_REPOS_ROOT
Directory the clones live under (default ~/GitHub). The CI pane asks gh about
the ones with a github.com origin. Legacy: \fBLEGHORN_ROOT\fR.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_SESSIONS_DIR
Claude Code's live-session markers, one <pid>.json per session (default
~/.claude/sessions). Legacy: \fBCCWORK_SESSIONS_DIR\fR.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_PROJECTS_DIR
Claude Code's JSONL transcripts, keyed by mangled cwd (default
~/.claude/projects). Legacy: \fBCLAUDE_PROJECTS_DIR\fR.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_CURSOR_HOME
Cursor's agent transcripts (default ~/.cursor). Legacy:
\fBCURSOR_AGENT_HOME\fR.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_CURSOR_MAX_IDLE_SECS
How far back a Cursor transcript still counts as a live agent (default 86400).
Legacy: \fBROOST_CURSOR_MAX_IDLE_SECS\fR.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_BACKENDS
Which discovery lanes run at all (default \fBclaude,cursor\fR). Legacy:
\fBROOST_BACKENDS\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gh (1),
.BR git (1)
